@* The app shell every authenticated page takes: ai-hub's top nav, ported
   from the prototype's HubNav.razor with issue #17. A translucent blurred
   strip over the brand gradient, the wordmark on the left, the signed-in
   Username and the round icon buttons on the right.

   /login does not come through here - it is a full-bleed gradient to the
   viewport edges and takes LoginLayout instead (docs/ROADMAP.md, "/login
   layout"). This shell is the authenticated chrome, and has a User. *@
@inherits LayoutComponentBase

<div class="hub-root">
    @* The band is the page's banner landmark; the nav inside it is the
       navigation. Why the gradient sits on the outer element is app.css
       section 6's business. *@
    <header class="hub-topband">
        <nav class="hub-nav">
            <a class="hub-nav-brand" href="@AppRoutes.Dashboard">@Constants.BrandName</a>

            <div class="hub-nav-actions">
                @* Inside the AuthorizeView with the Username, and not only
                   for symmetry: the router renders its "Not authorized" view
                   inside this layout, so anything outside here is chrome an
                   anonymous visitor is shown. Neither account action means
                   anything to them.

                   Links rather than buttons, because both destinations are
                   Static SSR pages (issue #24) and a page that must work
                   without a circuit cannot be reached by one. *@
                <AuthorizeView>
                    <Authorized>
                        <span class="hub-nav-user">@context.User.Identity!.Name</span>

                        <a class="hub-icon-btn" href="@AppRoutes.ChangePassword"
                           title="@Constants.ChangePassword.PageTitle" aria-label="@Constants.ChangePassword.PageTitle">
                            <HubIcon Name="@Constants.Icons.KeyRound" Size="@Constants.Icons.ButtonSize" />
                        </a>

                        <a class="hub-icon-btn" href="@AppRoutes.Logout"
                           title="@Constants.Logout.PageTitle" aria-label="@Constants.Logout.PageTitle">
                            <HubIcon Name="@Constants.Icons.LogOut" Size="@Constants.Icons.ButtonSize" />
                        </a>
                    </Authorized>
                </AuthorizeView>

                @* Pure JS, and it must stay that way: flipping the theme is
                   local UI state, which ADR 0003's amendment forbids
                   round-tripping. An @@onclick here would open a circuit and
                   bounce a click off the server to set one attribute.

                   Both icons render and CSS hides one - see theme.js for why
                   the toggle's contents are nobody else's business. *@
                <button type="button" class="hub-icon-btn" onclick="window.kosyncTheme.toggle()"
                        title="@Constants.ThemeToggleLabel" aria-label="@Constants.ThemeToggleLabel">
                    <span class="hub-theme-moon"><HubIcon Name="@Constants.Icons.Moon" Size="@Constants.Icons.ButtonSize" /></span>
                    <span class="hub-theme-sun"><HubIcon Name="@Constants.Icons.Sun" Size="@Constants.Icons.ButtonSize" /></span>
                </button>
            </div>
        </nav>
    </header>

    @* The prototype's nav also carried a "Manage accounts" icon button.
       Issue #28 built that page, and it is reached from the sidebar's
       Administration group (UsersNavGroup) instead - one link to one
       destination, rather than two entry points to be kept in step. *@
    <main class="hub-app-main">
        @Body
    </main>
</div>
